## DESCRIPTION
## Asks questions that tie a matrices determinant, invertibility, rref
## singularity and rank.
## ENDDESCRIPTION


## DBsubject(Linear algebra)
## DBchapter(Matrices)
## DBsection(Rank)
## Date(September 5, 2008)
## Institution(Fitchburg State University)
## Author(Peter Staab)
## KEYWORDS('matrix', 'determinant', 'rank','inverse', 'rref')

DOCUMENT();    # This should be the first executable line in the problem.
loadMacros(
	'PGstandard.pl',         'PGML.pl','PGmatrixmacros.pl', 
	'parserRadioButtons.pl', 'parserRadioMultiAnswer.pl'
);

Context('Vector');

do {
	$A = Matrix([
		[ random(-3, 3), random(-3, 3), 0 ],
		[ random(-3, 3), 0,             random(-3, 3) ],
		[ 0,             random(-3, 3), random(-3, 3) ]
	]);
	$det = $A->det;
} until abs($det) >0 && abs($det) <= 4;

do {
$x = ColumnVector([ random(-3, 3), random(-3, 3), random(-3, 3) ]);
} until $x->norm > 0;
$y = $A * $x;

#Find the inverse of the matrix:

$Ainv = $A->inverse;

$rma1 =
	RadioMultiAnswer([ [ 'The matrix \(A\) has inverse \(A^{-1}\) %s*', $Ainv ], ['\(A^{-1}\) does not exist'] ],
		0);

$rma2 = RadioMultiAnswer(
	[
		[ 'The solution to \(A\vec{x}=\vec{y}\) is \(\vec{x}=\) %s', $x ],
		['The solution to \(A\vec{x}=\vec{y}\) does not exist.']
	],
	0
);

$radio_rows = RadioButtons([ 'true', 'false' ], 'true',  separator => '&nbsp;');
$radio_cols = RadioButtons([ 'true', 'false' ], 'true',  separator => '&nbsp;');
$radio_sing = RadioButtons([ 'true', 'false' ], 'false', separator => '&nbsp;');

TEXT(beginproblem());
BEGIN_PGML

Let

[```  A= [$A]  \qquad \qquad \vec{y} = [$y]```]

1) [` \text{det}(A)= `] [____]{Real($det)}

2) [`\;`] [_____]{$rma1}

3) The reduced row echelon form of [`A`] is [_____]*{Matrix([[1,0,0],[0,1,0],[0,0,1]])}

4) [`\;`] [_____]{$rma2}

    Note: write your answer as a vector, i.e. <1,2,3> if it exists.

5) The rank of [` A `] is [___]{3}

6) The rows of [`A`] are linearly independent:  [____]{$radio_rows}

7) The columns of [`A`] are linearly independent: [____]{$radio_cols}

8) The matrix [` A `] is singular: [____]{$radio_sing}


END_PGML

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
This problem is supposed to show that many of the aspects of linear algebra are interrelated.
We'll go through each and explain how they are related. 

1. The determinant can either be found using row expansion or row reduction.  Using 
either of these shows [`|A| = [$det]`].
2. One can find the inverse by putting [`A \; | \; I`] into reduced row echelon form and
since the left half is the identity matrix, the right half is the inverse or 
[``` A^{-1} = [$Ainv] ```]

3. Putting the matrix in reduce row-echelon form results in the identity matrix or
[``` \begin{bmatrix} 1 & 0 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\ 0 & 0 & 1 \end{bmatrix} ```]

4. To find this, the easiest way is to row-reduce the following matrix:

    [``` \left[\begin{array}{rrr|r}
    [$A->element(1,1)] & [$A->element(1,2)] & [$A->element(1,3)] & [@ Matrix($y)->element(1) @] \\
    [$A->element(2,1)] & [$A->element(2,2)] & [$A->element(2,3)] & [@ Matrix($y)->element(2) @] \\
    [$A->element(3,1)] & [$A->element(3,2)] & [$A->element(3,3)] & [@ Matrix($y)->element(3) @] \\
    \end{array}\right]```]
    and the row-reduction matrix is
    [``` \left[\begin{array}{rrr|r}
    1 & 0 & 0 & [$x->{data}[0]] \\
    0 & 1 & 0 & [$x->{data}[1]] \\
    0 & 0 & 1 & [$x->{data}[2]]
    \end{array}\right]```]

    The solution is the rightmost column or 
    [``` \vec{x} = [$x]  ```]

5. The rank of a matrix [`A`] is the number of linearly independent rows.  This can 
be found from the number of non-zero rows of the row reduced form of [`A`], thus from
part 3, this can be found.  Thus, [`\text{rank}(A)=3`].

6. Because the rank is the same as the number of rows of [`A`], the rows are
linearly independent. 

7. Since the rows are  linearly independent, then the columns are as well.

8. Because the determinant of [`A`] is not zero, the matrix is **not** singular. 

END_PGML_SOLUTION
COMMENT("Asks related questions about a nonsingular matrix.");
ENDDOCUMENT();
